<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Removed for v1: com.apple.developer.healthkit (owner's decision, 2026-08-29).
	     It was added on 2026-08-17 as the entitlement the existing code had always needed and never
	     had - without it the authorisation callback comes back unsuccessful, quietly, and sleep data
	     simply never arrives. What the same pass did not check is whether anything could ask: the
	     only caller is MainViewModel.ImportSleepDataAsync, and no XAML binds it. The entitlement was
	     bought for a button that was never built.

	     What it cost to keep, and why that decided it: the App ID needs the HealthKit capability
	     enabled by hand or signing fails - CI cannot catch that, build-ios.yml publishes with
	     CodesignKey="-" - and App Review asks what health data is read and why. Two obligations,
	     one of them outside the repo, for a feature no user can reach.

	     Restoring it is one commit with four parts, and three of them are here in the repo:
	         <key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	         <true/>
	     plus NSHealthShareUsageDescription in Info.plist and both .lproj files, plus the HEALTHKIT
	     define in Diarion.csproj (which is what actually compiles the HealthKit code back in), plus
	     the capability on the App ID. And before any of it, a button: the read is read-only by
	     design - the share set passed to RequestAuthorizationToShare is empty, which is why there is
	     no NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription anywhere in this repo's history. -->

	<!-- Removed for v1: com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit.
	     It was added for the 1.1 GB generative model, and OnDeviceAi.GenerationOffered has been
	     false for everyone since 2026-08-10. What is left is the 123 MB encoder, which does not go
	     near the jetsam ceiling that entitlement was raising. An entitlement kept "just in case"
	     is not free: it has to be enabled on the App ID too, and a key in the binary that the
	     provisioning profile does not carry is a signing failure rather than a warning.
	     Restoring it is this, verbatim, in the same commit that flips GenerationOffered back:
	         <key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	         <true/>
	     It only ever took effect when the installing profile happened to carry it, so it bought
	     several hundred megabytes when it survived and nothing when it did not. -->
</dict>
</plist>
